  "notes": "GLITCH RUNS: glitch_standard_off registers FRAMEBUFFER_GLITCH with its state off under the standing shot table and compares against the same references as the default run, so it proves the off path byte-identical. Each glitch_* run captures one shot of the --glitch-test fixture (creations/demos/shape_debug/glitch_fixture.hpp); glitch_off is their control. SUBJECT GLITCH RUNS: glitch_subject_standard_off registers TRIXEL_GLITCH with no subject under the standing shot table and compares against the default references, so it proves the off path byte-identical. Each glitch_subject_* run captures the --glitch-subject-test fixture (creations/demos/shape_debug/glitch_subject_fixture.hpp); glitch_subject_off is their control and the spill_*_off rows are the far/near controls; its [glitch-subject-probe] log lines carry the structural evidence (CPU-twin mismatch, per-effect counts, the spill oracle, the choreography order). Shot order mirrors the kShots[] table in main.cpp. Each full-frame shot produces one screenshot_NNNNNN.png in order — the Nth entry here maps to the Nth full-frame screenshot by numeric index. This 'shots' list enumerates only the full-frame shots (render-verify's _collect_shots keeps crop PNGs out of the index mapping). Reference PNGs live in sibling subdirs named after the CMake preset (e.g. macos-debug/, linux-debug/) since GL and Metal produce pixel-different output. OPTIONAL GATE BLOCKS (epic #1766 T-2; both additive — omit for the original full-frame-only behavior): 'crops' = {shotLabel: [cropLabel, ...]} pixel-diffs each declared ROI crop (captured screenshot_NNNNNN_<shotLabel>__crop_<cropLabel>.png) against a committed reference crop named <shotLabel>__crop_<cropLabel>.png in the backend dir, using the same 'thresholds'; crops not listed here stay inspection-only. 'structural' = {shotLabel: [{metric, roi?, <threshold>...}]} runs the backend-agnostic render-<metric>-metric.py gate on that shot's full-frame capture — e.g. {metric: 'shadow', roi: [x,y,w,h], max_hole_ratio: 0.05, max_components: 8} invokes render-shadow-metric.py (#1765) with --roi/--max-hole-ratio/--max-components and fails the run on a non-zero exit. Threshold keys map to CLI flags by key.replace('_','-'); each structural gate needs at least one threshold key. Metrics: 'shadow' (#1765 shadow-overlay holes), and the T-3 suite (#1769) 'coverage' (interior background holes in a solid silhouette — the #1619 missing-face gate; --max-hole-ratio/--min-coverage/--max-hole-components), 'silhouette' (edge raggedness perimeter^2/area + component fragmentation; --max-perimeter-ratio/--max-components) and 'clip' (expected-bbox occupancy; --min-occupancy/--max-edge-touch-frac, pass the bbox via roi). An optional 'roi_at': [width, height] sibling of 'roi' records the capture size the roi's pixel coordinates were measured against; render-verify.py scales roi proportionally to each backend's actual capture size before invoking the metric script, so a roi authored on a HiDPI 2x macOS framebuffer (e.g. 2560x1440) still resolves correctly against a 1x linux-debug/windows-debug capture (e.g. 1280x720) instead of landing out-of-bounds (#3016). Omit roi_at when roi was authored directly against the demo's native (non-HiDPI) resolution — it is a no-op scale then. The three structural gates above are calibrated against the macos-debug references with generous margin (coverage clean=0.0 ratio, silhouette clean=17.45/1, clip clean=0.349 occupancy) so they pass both backends once DPI-scaled; tighten once linux-debug references land. Referencing an unimplemented metric fails loudly. The shape_debug crops (zoom4_origin / zoom8_origin / zoom8_yaw180) are not gated yet — they have no committed reference crops; opt them into 'crops' once references are captured via --update-references."
}
